---
# Admits monitor-bridge to bazarr's API, which the ingress default-deny otherwise refuses.
#
# `baseline-ingress` (netpol-baseline role) admits exactly four sources to every pod carrying
# `netpol-baseline: enforced`: traefik, prometheus from the observability namespace, and the two
# cni0 gateways. monitor-bridge is in none of them, so its Bazarr Health check got
# `Connection refused` on bazarr.homelab.svc.cluster.local:6767 on its first three cycles after
# the check shipped (2026-08-29) — the Service and its endpoint were correct throughout, and the
# pod answered fine on its own IP. A check that cannot reach its subject reports the fence, not
# the subject.
#
# Same shape and same reasoning as roles/k8s/prowlarr/templates/networkpolicy-prowlarr.yaml.j2.
# PORT-SCOPED: the grant is this one port, not the pod.
#
# traefik is listed deliberately and is NOT redundant to remove. While bazarr carries the
# baseline label the union is identical with or without it, because policies are additive. It
# stays so this policy states its own complete caller set rather than depending on the baseline
# continuing to admit traefik on every port — if that baseline rule is ever port-scoped, or this
# pod template loses the label, this line is what keeps the UI reachable.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: bazarr
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: bazarr
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: traefik
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: monitor-bridge
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ container_item.port }}
